.Dd September 24, 2026
.Dt PG_LASWELL_CITUS 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pg_laswell_citus
.Nd Citus support in pg_laswell: intent kinds, readings and refusals
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Citus support is a
.Em build-time module .
The core binary speaks PostgreSQL and nothing else; a binary that carries this
module additionally knows the kinds below, takes an extra reading of the
cluster's topology, and refuses several things a stock build would accept and
then fail to execute.
.Pp
This page describes a binary built with the module.
To tell whether you have one:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ pg_laswell_mcp --version
0.1.2
modules: citus
.Ed
.Pp
A binary with no
.Li modules
line does not recognise any kind on this page, and will refuse a specification
that names one -- the whole specification, not the one intent, which is the same
rule that governs any unknown kind.
Build one with:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cmake -S cpp -B build -DPGLASWELL_MODULES=citus
.Ed
.Pp
Every kind here is prefixed
.Cm citus_ ,
so a specification says which vendor it needs and two vendors cannot collide on
a name.
The
.Em MODULES
section of
.Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1
gives the rules a module works under, and what it is forbidden to do.
.Sh INTENT KINDS
.\" BEGIN CITUS KIND LIST -- checked by tools/check-manual.py against
.\" cpp/src/modules/citus/kinds.inc.
Every kind accepts an optional
.Va comment :
a note for whoever reads the specification.
None of these kinds creates an object, so it is not written to the catalog.
.Ss Cm citus_distribute_table
Hash-distribute a table across the cluster's worker nodes.
.Pp
Required:
.Va schema ,
.Va table ,
.Va distribution_column .
Optional:
.Va comment ,
.Va shard_method ,
.Va colocate_with ,
.Va shard_count ,
.Va concurrently .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va distribution_column
The column whose hash picks the shard.
.Em Every
unique constraint and primary key on the table must include it: Citus cannot
enforce uniqueness across shards without it, so it refuses the distribution
rather than the constraint.
An intent whose table has a key not covering this column is refused, naming the
key and the columns it does cover.
.It Va shard_method
.Cm hash
(the default),
.Cm append
or
.Cm range .
Hash is the only form Citus recommends for new work; the other two are legacy.
.It Va colocate_with
.Qq schema.table ,
or one of Citus's own words
.Cm none
and
.Cm default .
An unqualified name is refused as ambiguous with those sentinels, so a table
genuinely called
.Li none
is written
.Qq public.none .
Colocating requires the named table to be distributed already -- in an earlier
intent of the same specification, which is accounted for, or an earlier
specification ordered by
.Va depends_on .
.It Va shard_count
A positive integer.
Mutually exclusive with a real
.Va colocate_with :
joining a colocation group adopts that group's shard count, so naming both asks
for two different numbers.
Refused at parse time rather than half-applied at execution.
.It Va concurrently
.Cm auto
(the default),
.Cm always
or
.Cm never .
Under
.Cm auto
the planner chooses from the table's estimated size and records which reading
decided.
.Cm always
requires
.Fn create_distributed_table_concurrently ,
which needs a replica identity -- a table with no primary key cannot use it --
and is refused, not silently downgraded, on a Citus too old to have it.
Someone who wrote
.Cm always
was making a decision about a table too big to lock, and quietly locking it
anyway is the worst available answer.
.El
.Ss Cm citus_create_reference_table
Replicate a table in full to every node, so reads join against it locally.
.Pp
Required:
.Va schema ,
.Va table .
Optional:
.Va comment .
No other keys are accepted.
.Pp
Always warns: every write to a reference table becomes a two-phase commit across
all nodes.
Reference tables are for small, rarely written lookups; a busy one is a
cluster-wide bottleneck.
.Ss Cm citus_distribute_function
Route calls of a function to the node holding the relevant shard, or replicate
its definition to every node.
.Pp
Required:
.Va schema ,
.Va name ,
.Va arguments .
Optional:
.Va comment ,
.Va distribution_argument ,
.Va colocate_with ,
.Va force_delegation .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va arguments
The argument type list that identifies the overload, as PostgreSQL writes it.
.It Va distribution_argument Ns / Ns Va colocate_with
These travel together and either both appear or neither does.
The argument says which parameter routes the call; the colocation target says
which table's shards it routes to.
One without the other cannot be acted on, and is refused.
.It Va force_delegation
Boolean.
.El
.Pp
With neither colocation key, the function is replicated to every node rather
than routed, and the plan warns: every node gets a copy of the definition, and a
later
.Ic CREATE OR REPLACE
has to be propagated too or the copies diverge.
.Pp
The function must already exist; create it in an earlier specification and order
the two with
.Va depends_on .
.Ss Cm citus_alter_distributed_table
Change how a distributed table is distributed: its column, its shard count, or
the group it is colocated with.
.Pp
Required:
.Va schema ,
.Va table ,
and at least one of
.Va distribution_column ,
.Va shard_count
and
.Va colocate_with .
Optional:
.Va cascade_to_colocated ,
.Va comment .
.Pp
Citus rewrites the table: it creates a new one, copies every row, drops the old
one and renames.
That is a full copy under an exclusive lock, with no concurrent form, and the
plan always warns with the estimated row count.
.Pp
Each property is compared with the reading, and only what differs is passed to
Citus.
When nothing differs, the step is
.Em satisfied
and no call is made.
Citus refuses a call that changes nothing
.Pq Qq this call doesn't change any properties of the table ,
so without this check a specification whose change had already happened would
fail when run again.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va distribution_column
Every unique key must include the new column, as for
.Cm citus_distribute_table .
Citus checks this only after it has copied the data.
Cannot be combined with
.Va cascade_to_colocated : Cm true ;
Citus cascades only the shard count.
.It Va shard_count
When other tables share the group,
.Va cascade_to_colocated
must be written.
.Cm true
rewrites every member with the new count and keeps the group, and its foreign
keys, together;
.Cm false
moves this table out of the group on its own.
Cannot be combined with a real
.Va colocate_with :
joining a group adopts its count.
.It Va colocate_with
.Qq schema.table
to join that table's group, which must be distributed on a column of exactly the
same type, or
.Cm none
for a group of its own.
A table that is already alone is satisfied for
.Cm none .
Citus would otherwise rewrite it into a new group for nothing.
.Cm default
is refused: whether a table is already in Citus's default group cannot be read
beforehand.
.El
.Pp
.Sy Foreign keys that would be dropped are refused.
Moving a table out of its colocation group while a foreign key joins it to
another distributed table
.Em succeeds
in Citus, with only a warning, and the constraint is gone.
The plan refuses any change that breaks colocation while such a key exists.
Keys to and from reference tables survive the rewrite and are not refused.
.Ss Cm citus_undistribute_table
Turn a distributed, reference or Citus-managed local table back into an ordinary
table on the coordinator.
.Pp
Required:
.Va schema ,
.Va table .
Optional:
.Va cascade_via_foreign_keys ,
.Va comment .
.Pp
Every row is copied onto the coordinator under an exclusive lock.
The coordinator needs the disk space for all of it, and the plan warns with the
estimated row count.
A table Citus does not record is
.Em satisfied .
.Pp
When a foreign key joins the table to another table Citus records, in either
direction, Citus refuses to undistribute one end alone.
.Va cascade_via_foreign_keys : Cm true
undistributes every table the keys connect, and the plan names them; without it
the plan is refused.
.Pp
There is no kind that reverses
.Cm citus_distribute_function :
Citus has no call that does.
.Ss Cm citus_add_local_table_to_metadata
Record a local table in Citus metadata without distributing it.
Its rows stay on the coordinator, and it can then hold foreign keys to and from
reference tables and join with them.
.Pp
Required:
.Va schema ,
.Va table .
Optional:
.Va cascade_via_foreign_keys ,
.Va comment .
.Pp
Satisfied when the table is already a Citus-managed local table.
Refused when it is distributed or a reference table, which Citus rejects as
.Qq already distributed .
A foreign key to a table Citus does not record needs
.Va cascade_via_foreign_keys : Cm true ,
which adds the connected tables as well.
A key to a reference table is what this kind is for, and needs no cascade.
.Ss Cm citus_truncate_local_data
Delete the coordinator's own copy of a distributed or reference table's rows.
.Fn create_distributed_table
leaves that copy in place after copying the rows to the shards.
.Pp
Required:
.Va schema ,
.Va table .
Optional:
.Va comment .
.Pp
Calls
.Fn truncate_local_data_after_distributing_table .
The rows Citus serves are in the shards and are not touched.
.Pp
.Sy The call truncates with CASCADE.
A table that references this one and is not distributed has its
.Em own
rows deleted: a local table referencing a reference table was emptied.
The plan is refused while any such table references it.
A distributed table referencing it is allowed: only the coordinator's stale copy
of it is truncated, and Citus still served every row.
.Ss Cluster kinds
The kinds below change the cluster, not a table.
They name nodes, and a node exists on one cluster only:
.Li worker1:5432
on staging is not a host anywhere else.
A specification that uses them describes that topology and is bound to the
clusters it exists on.
.Cm citus_ensure_workers
takes its hosts from the configuration instead, and
.Cm citus_rebalance_shards
names nothing: both run on any cluster.
.Pp
Each takes
.Va host
and an optional
.Va port
(default 5432).
Node membership is projected the way table state is: an intent sees the nodes an
earlier intent of the same specification adds, removes, drains or changes, so
registering the coordinator, setting its property and adding the workers can be
one specification, in that order.
The planner does not reorder intents.
One thing cannot be projected: how many shards a rebalance will move after
membership changes, since Citus decides that when it runs.
A
.Cm citus_rebalance_shards
after such a change is therefore always applied, and its plan says the number
is unknown.
.Pp
No kind moves or splits a single shard.
Shard ids come from a sequence in each database, so they depend on everything
created there before \(em measured, the same table built by the same
specifications starts at shard 102140 in one database and 102107 in another on
the same cluster.
A signed specification naming a shard would mean a different shard, or none, on
every other target.
Draining and rebalancing say what is wanted and leave the choice of shards to
Citus.
.Ss Cm citus_ensure_workers
Make the cluster's workers the ones this connection's configuration lists, so a
specification says
.Em what
the cluster should be and names no host.
The same signed file then adds three workers on a staging cluster and twelve in
production:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{ "kind": "citus_ensure_workers",
  "hosts_matching": "*.citus.internal",
  "min_workers": 2 }
.Ed
.Pp
The list comes from
.Va citus.workers
in the connection's section of the configuration file, a comma-separated list of
.Ar host
or
.Ar host : Ns Ar port
(port 5432 by default); see
.Sx CONFIGURATION
in
.Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1 .
A connection with none is refused, saying where to put it.
.Pp
Optional:
.Va hosts_matching ,
.Va min_workers ,
.Va max_workers ,
.Va unlisted ,
.Va comment .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va hosts_matching
A glob that every configured host must match, with
.Ql *
for any run of characters and
.Ql \&?
for one, and nothing else.
.It Va min_workers Ns / Ns Va max_workers
Bounds on how many workers the configuration may list.
.It Va unlisted
What happens to a registered worker the configuration does not list:
.Cm keep
(the default) leaves it and warns,
.Cm refuse
stops the plan, and
.Cm remove
removes it, which is refused while it still holds shards of a distributed table.
.El
.Pp
.Sy The bounds are the reason to sign this kind rather than trust the file.
The configuration is not signed, and a worker receives shards of the data, so
without them whoever can edit the configuration decides which machines hold it.
A configured host outside
.Va hosts_matching ,
or a count outside the bounds, is refused, not warned.
.Pp
Satisfied when every configured worker is registered and active and nothing is
to be removed.
Each worker is added with
.Fn citus_add_node ,
which connects to it, and the dry run executes the call, so an unreachable
configured host is reported before the migration.
Refused when the list names the coordinator, a host twice, or a worker that is
registered and inactive.
New workers hold no shards until the cluster is rebalanced, which a
.Cm citus_rebalance_shards
later in the same specification does.
.Ss Cm citus_add_node
Add a worker.
Like every kind that names a node, it is bound to the clusters where that host
exists; see
.Sx Cluster kinds .
Optional:
.Va should_have_shards
(boolean; false adds a node that takes no shards).
Satisfied when the node is already registered and active; refused when it is
registered and inactive.
The call connects to the node while it runs, so the dry run executes it and an
unreachable host is reported before the migration.
A new node holds nothing until the cluster is rebalanced.
.Ss Cm citus_remove_node
Remove a worker.
Satisfied when it is not registered.
Refused for the coordinator, and for a node that still holds shards of
distributed tables \(em Citus refuses to remove the only copy of a shard.
Drain it first, in an earlier intent or specification.
.Ss Cm citus_set_coordinator_host
Register the coordinator's address, the one the workers use to reach it.
Satisfied when the coordinator is already registered at that address.
.Ss Cm citus_set_node_property
Required:
.Va should_have_shards
(boolean), the only property Citus lets you set.
Satisfied when it already has that value.
Setting it to false stops new shards arriving and moves none; the plan says how
many the node keeps.
.Ss Cm citus_drain_node
Move every shard of a distributed table off a node, and mark it as taking no new
ones.
Optional:
.Va transfer_mode .
Satisfied when the node holds no such shards and takes no new ones.
Refused when no other node may hold shards.
Reference-table placements stay on the node; a drain does not move them.
.Pp
Not atomic: the node is marked as taking no shards before anything moves, and
each move commits on its own, so the step runs outside a transaction and a dry
run does not execute it.
A failed drain keeps what it moved, and running the specification again
finishes it.
See
.Sx Moving shards .
.Ss Cm citus_rebalance_shards
Move shards until Citus's own rebalancer has nothing left to do.
Optional:
.Va transfer_mode .
Satisfied when the plan from
.Fn get_rebalance_table_shards_plan
is empty; otherwise the plan lists how many moves there are and the first
twenty.
Runs
.Fn rebalance_table_shards ,
which returns only when the rebalance is finished, so
.Qq applied
in the ledger means balanced.
The background form,
.Fn citus_rebalance_start ,
would leave a job the ledger knows nothing about.
.Pp
Not atomic: each move commits on its own, so the step runs outside a
transaction and a dry run does not execute it.
A failed rebalance keeps what it moved, and running the specification again
finishes it.
See
.Sx Moving shards .
.Ss Moving shards
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va transfer_mode
.Cm auto
(the default) or
.Cm force_logical
copy each shard by logical replication while writes continue; they need
.Li wal_level = logical
on every worker, which the plan reads from the workers themselves and refuses
without.
.Cm block_writes
holds writes to each shard while it is copied, and needs no replication.
.It Neither drain nor rebalance is atomic.
Measured: inside a transaction that was then rolled back,
.Fn rebalance_table_shards
moved a shard and kept it moved, and
.Fn citus_drain_node
marked the node as taking no shards, and kept that mark, even when the drain
itself then failed.
Every move commits on its own.
So both run outside a transaction and the dry run does not execute them: it lists
them as unverified.
A failed drain or rebalance keeps the moves it made, and running the
specification again carries on from there.
.El
.\" END CITUS KIND LIST
.Sh READINGS
With the module built in, every plan against a database where the
.Li citus
extension is installed carries an extra reading under
.Va extensions.citus :
the extension version, the worker count, which distributed tables exist with
their distribution column and colocation group, which functions are distributed,
whether this Citus has
.Fn create_distributed_table_concurrently ,
and the values of
.Va citus.enable_ddl_propagation
and
.Va citus.max_adaptive_executor_pool_size .
.Pp
An absent reading is not a zero reading.
A database with no Citus installed produces no
.Va extensions.citus
at all, and every kind on this page is refused there with that as the reason,
rather than planned against defaults.
.Pp
Contention is read differently too.
On a coordinator,
.Va pg_locks
shows the coordinator's own waits and not a worker's, so a shard-level wait is
invisible where a single-node reading would find it.
Where the module applies, waiters come from
.Va citus_lock_waits
and activity from
.Va citus_stat_activity ,
which see the whole cluster.
.Sh WRITES ON A DISTRIBUTED TABLE
A specification does not name its vendor to do an ordinary thing.
The same
.Cm backfill
runs on plain PostgreSQL in development and on a distributed table in staging;
the binary adapts per target through what it reads.
Measured on Citus 13, preparing and executing each statement against a
distributed table:
.Bl -column "delete_rows by predicate" "grouped walk" -offset indent
.It Sy form Ta Sy outcome
.It Cm insert_rows Ta runs
.It Cm copy_rows Ta runs Pq Citus routes COPY itself
.It Cm update_rows Ta runs
.It Cm delete_rows No by values Ta runs
.It Cm backfill Ta grouped walk
.It Cm delete_rows No by predicate Ta grouped walk
.It Cm merge_rows Ta refused
.El
.Pp
The first four take their rows from the specification and join to them; they
take no row locks, so nothing about them is distributed-specific.
.Pp
The two walks select each batch
.Ic FOR UPDATE ,
which stops the keyset cursor selecting the same rows twice, and Citus refuses
that on a multi-shard query.
So core asks this module one question -- must row-locking batches on this table
be confined to single values of a column, and which? -- and for a distributed
table the answer is its distribution column.
Core then walks one value at a time: which values remain, which keys remain
inside one, and the change to exactly those keys, each confined by an equality on
the distribution column.
That is the only confinement Citus pushes down; a hash-range predicate is not,
reads every shard, and is refused the same way.
The write comes back
.Li Task Count: 1 .
.Pp
The step records
.Va batch_mode
as
.Cm grouped
and
.Va confined_by
as
.Cm citus ,
and its reason says the Citus reading decided.
The cursor records both positions, as
.Ql ["3","800"] ,
and a walk that fails on one value resumes after the last committed batch --
verified by failing one tenant partway and retrying.
.Pp
For
.Cm backfill
the key must be unique
.Em within
one distribution value, which a unique index on
.Pq Va distribution column , Va key
in that order proves.
A unique index on the key alone cannot exist on a distributed table: Citus
refuses one that does not contain the distribution column.
.Pp
The gain is not speed.
A shard-ordered walk measured about 16% better per row than a key-ordered one,
not the eightfold a first measurement suggested.
The reason to have it is that on a distributed table the alternative is not a
slower walk, it is no walk at all.
.Sh REFUSALS
These are the reason the module exists: each is a Citus error that would
otherwise surface at execution, partway through a migration, on a cluster, with
earlier work already committed.
All of them are derivable from the readings above beforehand.
.Ss Topology-wide
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va citus.enable_ddl_propagation Li is off
Refused for
.Em every
kind, core kinds included -- a plain
.Cm create_table
on a Citus cluster is propagated DDL too.
With propagation off, DDL runs on the coordinator and not on the workers, and
the two catalogs diverge
.Em silently :
every reading pg_laswell takes, including the check that catches drift, is taken
on the coordinator.
The ledger would record the migration as fully applied, the coordinator would
agree, and the shards would not have it.
A warning would be a false reassurance; refusing is recoverable in one
statement.
.It Cm merge_rows No on a distributed table
Refused, for a reason that has nothing to do with pacing: Citus restricts MERGE
against a distributed target.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ERROR: non-IMMUTABLE functions are not yet supported in MERGE sql
       with distributed tables
.Ed
.Pp
Express the change as
.Cm update_rows
and
.Cm insert_rows ,
which both run.
.It A walk whose key is the distribution column
.Cm backfill
and
.Cm delete_rows
by predicate walk a distributed table one distribution value at a time (see
.Sx WRITES ON A DISTRIBUTED TABLE ) .
When the distribution column is the walk key, every value holds one row and every
batch would be a single row, so the walk is refused and the refusal names what
runs instead.
.El
.Ss Per kind
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
The
.Li citus
extension is not installed in this database.
.It
A unique constraint or primary key does not include the distribution column.
.It
The distribution column does not exist on the table.
.It
.Va colocate_with
names a table this database does not record as distributed.
.It
.Va concurrently : Cm always
was asked for and this Citus has no concurrent form.
.It
.Va concurrently : Cm always
on a table with no replica identity.
.It
A function named for distribution does not exist.
.It
.Va shard_count
together with a real
.Va colocate_with ,
or
.Va distribution_argument
without
.Va colocate_with .
.It
An alter that changes a shard count in a shared group without
.Va cascade_to_colocated ,
or that would silently drop a foreign key to another distributed table.
.It
Undistributing, or adding to metadata, one end of a foreign key without
.Va cascade_via_foreign_keys .
.It
Truncating local data that would cascade into a table's real rows.
.It
Removing a node that holds shards, or the coordinator.
.It
Configured workers outside the specification's
.Va hosts_matching ,
.Va min_workers
or
.Va max_workers ,
or a connection with no
.Va citus.workers
at all.
.It
Draining a node when no other node may hold shards.
.It
Moving shards by logical replication when a worker is not at
.Li wal_level = logical .
.It
Rebalancing when fewer nodes may hold shards than the replication factor
requires, where Citus cannot compute a plan at all.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Distribute a parent, then colocate a child with it, in one specification.
The second intent is planned against the state the first will have produced, so
it does not need a separate deployment:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
  "laswell_spec_version": 1,
  "id": "0030-distribute",
  "description": "Distribute accounts and colocate its ledger.",
  "intents": [
    { "kind": "citus_distribute_table",
      "schema": "public", "table": "accounts",
      "distribution_column": "id",
      "comment": "Hash distributed on id." },
    { "kind": "citus_distribute_table",
      "schema": "public", "table": "account_transactions",
      "distribution_column": "account_id",
      "colocate_with": "public.accounts",
      "comment": "Colocated with accounts." }
  ]
}
.Ed
.Pp
A runnable three-node cluster and the specifications above are in
.Pa examples/docker/citus
in the source.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pg_laswell 1 ,
.Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1
.Pp
.Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1
is the authoritative reference for the specification format, pacing, trust and
the ledger; this page covers only what the Citus module adds.
